Have user enter current 1rm on account creation.


Referencing conjugate powerlifting best practices at https://www.westside-barbell.com/blogs/the-blog, and the above schemas, generate a psuedocode algorithm to generate a conjugate powerlifting workout program.  The program should have an option to be either 2 days, 3 days, or 4 days long.  2 and 3 day length programs benefit from Phil Daru's condensed conjugate approach to training.

My current though on the algorithm is as follows

1. fetch all exercises
2. fetch all exercise muscle
3. fetch all exercise equipment
4. get the user's program over their program length
5. foreach exercise in structure
    a. filter to set of available exercises based on (muscle, equipment) constraints specified in program.
    b. sort based on (num equipment options desc, targeted muscles desc, exercise name)
        i. This gives consistent ordering for program rotation, to allow for even spacing between the time each exercise is done.
    c. check program length window
        i. if the number of available exercises is less than the program length window, continue
        ii. else, remove all exercises from the available set that have already been completed over the program lookback window.
    d. select the next avaiable exercise
    e. select sets, reps, weight based on exercise movement type and how many weeks you are into program (for de workout weight %)

The above algorithm would also need to be updated to take into account a user's exercise preferences (exercises to include or avoid), and a user's available equipment.  The algorithm should also be designed to select accessory exercises that focus on a user's weak muscles, which we can assume we have access to.

ensure that as exercises are selected, the exercises for each category are cycled through completely before repeating any exercise from a category.  This check should occur after the exercises have been filtered based on a user's preferences, available equipment, etc...

Handle the case when a user doesn't have any history too.  Also update to use prilepin's chart for selecting number of repititions, based on whether a movement is a primary movement or not.  Outside of exercise type, the other variables that can be modified when programming an exercise are number of sets/repititions/tempo, and these should also meet the guidelines in prilepin's chart.  Other rep schemes include amrap (as many reps as possible), emom (every minute on the minute), For all exercises, also output the following information:

is_amrap BOOLEAN NOT NULL (is as many reps as possible)
is_emom BOOLEAN NOT NULL,
use_tempo BOOLEAN NOT NULL,
eccentric_tempo CHAR(1) NOT NULL,
isometric_tempo CHAR(1) NOT NULL,
concentric_tempo CHAR(1) NOT NULL,
target_weight REAL NOT NULL,
target_rep_count SMALLINT NOT NULL,

Obtain the target weight based on a user's exercise history, and if not specified their current 1rm (one rep maximum).
Handle the case when a first week  of workouts is generated and a user doesn't have any exercise history as well.

Only 1 week of workouts should be generated at once.

Incorporate the concept of undulating periodization from conjugate powerlifting into the workout generation algorithm as well.

Use all the existing models that we have implemented.  Prefer updating existing models over adding new models.

Add this functionality as a service since it is higher-level than a dal.

Add comprehensive unit tests to make sure edge cases with missing data in various parts of the algorithm are robustly handled.  Also include comprehensive integration tests.

